#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "abb40124-5eda-57b4-a815-9cd8b5661da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5389" :question/problem "Plot the graph of the polar curve $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/8c25245d58ec9ddd8d8f1e73f8eae12c244ecd32018f939f1aba86b274622ba8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "6272bafb-8e57-583f-a320-f066fe40184b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5390" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/245f03c8eeb5b84f7b4caafd61f37b7c15e77e77a447fc762170e05d61c87864.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line That Passes Through a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "04f489ae-768f-5e8e-bed6-0a88f481c54c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5391" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/1e1362c20f9e20bb22d049d8b218d786a5254603ab4cc36d90b5866095a8897c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/id #uuid "e2c565be-0152-5088-8c4f-e66374cf2fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5392" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/728f7aad12a4f127cc002bc38c46fa87ba70d6ecd79ef311a0154014b649a0ce.png)" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20819b41-67a2-5b7f-9867-e375985fe85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{2},-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307699} {:answer/id #uuid "36e43af0-4e3b-57f7-b547-8abf5f44af31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307700} {:answer/id #uuid "913ea399-1fc5-5f4a-a21f-1193ef384609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307701} {:answer/id #uuid "a9730ffb-ade1-58b4-ac44-f48b23b73db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8\\sqrt{3},-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307702} {:answer/id #uuid "56f7130b-d735-5df5-bf6a-d79ea5239c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12\\sqrt{3},-12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36e43af0-4e3b-57f7-b547-8abf5f44af31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307700} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f291c82-fb61-573e-b69d-b8bb4bc2eed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9300a06e-224a-5760-bf8d-1bf574e7c275" :question/math-academy-id "q-120719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/3b1318b156281c7bc8724f988545d9f5c9fab2bda4ed88c387f051dbe5bece1e.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=12$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(12,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6\\sqrt{3},-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4650445f-fe67-5c51-9c97-951a7acb0bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{26\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280830} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2ac67e-7b7e-5108-a0c2-4421dcf0e882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280831} {:answer/id #uuid "e38a772b-0f31-5524-aca3-b31e22edfc74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280832} {:answer/id #uuid "bba5ee2e-5aa4-51b6-9ef9-21e29b1837eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280833} {:answer/id #uuid "8539c6fb-0486-5bc7-a6b1-fb6f88e1f3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba5ee2e-5aa4-51b6-9ef9-21e29b1837eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280833} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b075980-b8ae-55d6-b30c-765aa3eba0ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5a952bc-ebaf-5525-bf7b-5b10a4e51c46" :question/math-academy-id "q-17151" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/4a908a59498e1bccef0601ce813ef0a4fa2b7e965c944c9951b13a9f50256459.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi +\\frac{4\\pi }{15}=\\frac{19\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{15}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c76a8e01-64ec-54cc-80fe-0d17a74a6292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/q-40827-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131919} {:answer/id #uuid "f53b07be-08f9-573e-9a5d-7be93a649963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/q-40827-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131920} {:answer/id #uuid "014ffb84-00eb-5ee2-ae2a-7292acd0253b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/q-40827-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131921} {:answer/id #uu